\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RpppFinalConjsentVar
\item[* relevant examples:] we are against noisy neighbours, although we 
don't live 
here yet; You are in for this job, without considering all the details of 
your application.
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] IdentPropFormation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_pppSentAdvVar
\item[* date of creation:] 9-MAR-1989 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] RIdentConjsentVar
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 24/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
1) there is only one var-extraction order in analysis (i.e.\ tempadv - locadv - 
sentadv), correlated with the actual surface order of the vars (which is 
sentadv (momentarily only in leftdislocrel) - locadv - tempadv), so there is 
right to left extraction)\\
2) in generation, there are more orders possible for insertion (depending on 
source language)\\
3) the extraction order for elements of the same class remains the same over 
languages, with for locadvs and conjsents/sentadvs obligatory left to right 
insertion 
(locadv1 - locadv2 - locadv3 etc) = right to left extraction, and for tempadvs 
(which have a different surface order in Dutch) the other way around. 
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:]
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) There may
be more than one postsentadvrel 
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\
Dutch assumed an attribute {\em distri\/}(bution) for CONJs, indicating 
whether a CONJ can occur sentence initially and/or finally. In the substitution
rules (where the CONJ is visible), there is a test on correct occurrence. For
English, no examples could be found of CONJs restricted to final use (they all 
seem to be allowed initially), but this condition has still been built in 
(see the prop subst rules).
\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
Note that the ordering 
of conjsents may be relevant for pronominal reference; therefore, the strategy 
in IL is to preserve the ordering of (conj)sents as much as possible,
i.e. the propsubst rule for an initial conjsent is mapped to the propsubst rule 
for an initial conjsent in other languages 
as well. Dutch also has a `middle' conjsent; since this seems unfelicitous
in English, it is mapped onto an initial conjsent, and with a lowered bonus to 
a final one.
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}


